  "Test for rate limiting on notification/subscription endpoints": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a rate-limited endpoint (hit it until 429) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try bypassing the limit by rotating the client key the limiter uses:\nX-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4\nX-Real-IP: 1.2.3.4\nX-Client-IP / X-Forwarded-Host\n3. Also vary: case/trailing slash in path, HTTP version, new session/token per request, and different API version paths.\n4. Use Burp Intruder / Turbo Intruder to measure true throughput vs the stated limit.\n5. Confirm you can exceed the intended limit.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe rate limit is keyed on a spoofable value (IP header) or bypassable via path/version/session variation, so brute force and abuse still work.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test for rate limiting on data export endpoints": "Steps:\n\n1. Identify the sensitive endpoint (data export) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Send a rapid burst of requests (Burp Intruder / a script) and count how many succeed before any 429/lockout.\n3. Check whether limiting is per-user (better) or per-IP, and whether it resets too quickly.\n4. For auth/OTP/reset endpoints, no limit = brute force; for data endpoints = scraping/DoS.\n5. Confirm the absence (or ineffectiveness) of rate limiting.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe endpoint enforces no effective rate limiting, enabling brute force, credential attacks, scraping, or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test for Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA) in API": "Steps:\n\n1. Create two accounts (A and B). As A, perform the operation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Find the object identifier in the request (path/query/body/header), e.g. /api/resource/{id}.\n3. Swap A’s object id for B’s and replay the GET request.\n4. If you read/modify/delete B’s object, object-level authorization is missing (BOLA/IDOR).\n5. Try id formats: sequential ints, UUIDs harvested from responses, encoded ids, and nested ids.\n6. Confirm cross-user access and note the data/action exposed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API checks that the object exists but not that the caller owns it, so changing the id on a GET request accesses another user’s resource (BOLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for excessive data exposure in API response objects": "Steps:\n\n1. Call a normal read endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Inspect the raw JSON response (not just what the UI shows) for extra fields: password hashes, tokens, internal ids, PII, flags.\n3. APIs often return the full object and rely on the client to hide fields.\n4. Check list/search endpoints too — they may leak other users’ data in bulk.\n5. Confirm and document sensitive fields exposed beyond what’s needed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API returns more data than the client needs (internal fields, PII, secrets), relying on client-side filtering — excessive data exposure.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test for mass assignment via API JSON body parameters": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a create/update API request and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Enumerate extra parameters the API might bind: role, isAdmin, permission, group, verified, status, price, owner, id.\n3. Add these fields to the JSON body one by one with privileged values and resend.\n4. Check via a follow-up read whether any injected field was persisted.\n5. Try nested objects, alternate casing, and duplicate keys to bypass naive filters.\n6. Confirm impact (privilege escalation, price/ownership tampering) with the modified object.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API auto-binds request-body fields to the object without an allow-list, letting an attacker set sensitive attributes (mass assignment / over-posting).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for API response containing unnecessary PII data": "Steps:\n\n1. Call a normal read endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Inspect the raw JSON response (not just what the UI shows) for extra fields: password hashes, tokens, internal ids, PII, flags.\n3. APIs often return the full object and rely on the client to hide fields.\n4. Check list/search endpoints too — they may leak other users’ data in bulk.\n5. Confirm and document sensitive fields exposed beyond what’s needed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API returns more data than the client needs (internal fields, PII, secrets), relying on client-side filtering — excessive data exposure.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test for API response with internal IDs and references": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s intended audience/permission and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Attempt the action as an unauthorized user (no auth, other user, lower role).\n3. Tamper object ids and try privileged methods/endpoints.\n4. Compare authorized vs unauthorized responses to detect missing checks.\n5. Confirm and document the access-control gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API endpoint fails to enforce proper authorization, allowing access/actions the caller isn’t entitled to (broken access control).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for API response with other users data leakage": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s intended audience/permission and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Attempt the action as an unauthorized user (no auth, other user, lower role).\n3. Tamper object ids and try privileged methods/endpoints.\n4. Compare authorized vs unauthorized responses to detect missing checks.\n5. Confirm and document the access-control gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API endpoint fails to enforce proper authorization, allowing access/actions the caller isn’t entitled to (broken access control).\n\nSeverity: High",
