  "Verify if API scopes in OAuth tokens are enforced on each endpoint": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the OAuth2 flow (authorize/token endpoints) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control: client_secret validation on token requests, scope enforcement, refresh-token rotation, redirect_uri validation, PKCE.\n3. Try tampering scope, reusing refresh tokens, and using loose redirect_uri matches to capture codes/tokens.\n4. Check whether tokens are bound to the client and can’t be replayed elsewhere.\n5. Confirm any weakness that yields excess scope or token theft.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe OAuth2 implementation weakly validates client credentials/scope/redirect_uri/refresh tokens, enabling scope escalation or token theft.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API authentication works correctly with both JSON and form-urlencoded bodies": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s auth/authorization requirement and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control in this check (auth enforcement, token validation, authorization).\n3. Attempt access without auth, with another user’s token, and with a tampered token.\n4. Compare responses to detect missing/weak enforcement.\n5. Confirm and document the authentication/authorization gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s authentication/authorization control described here is missing or weak, allowing unauthorized access.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Check if preflight OPTIONS requests do not bypass authentication": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s auth/authorization requirement and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control in this check (auth enforcement, token validation, authorization).\n3. Attempt access without auth, with another user’s token, and with a tampered token.\n4. Compare responses to detect missing/weak enforcement.\n5. Confirm and document the authentication/authorization gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s authentication/authorization control described here is missing or weak, allowing unauthorized access.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API token is transmitted only over secure HTTPS connections": "Steps:\n\n1. Check whether the token can be sent over plain HTTP.\n2. Request the http:// version of an API endpoint with the token and see if it’s accepted (no forced HTTPS/HSTS).\n3. Capture traffic to confirm the token would traverse the network in cleartext.\n4. Any plaintext transmission exposes the token to network interception.\n5. Confirm missing HTTPS enforcement / HSTS for token-bearing requests.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI tokens can be transmitted over unencrypted HTTP, exposing them to network interception.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Verify if API rejects expired tokens with proper error codes": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain a token and decode it (for JWT: paste into jwt.io or `base64 -d` the payload).\n2. Check the exp claim / documented lifetime. Look for missing exp or a very long TTL (days/months/never).\n3. Test whether an old token still works long after issuance / after logout.\n4. A non-expiring or long-lived token stays valid if stolen, with a huge abuse window.\n5. Confirm the token doesn’t expire promptly or isn’t revoked on logout.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI tokens have no (or an excessively long) expiration and/or aren’t revoked on logout, so a stolen token remains usable for a long time.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test if authentication middleware processes requests before authorization checks": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s intended audience/permission and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Attempt the action as an unauthorized user (no auth, other user, lower role).\n3. Tamper object ids and try privileged methods/endpoints.\n4. Compare authorized vs unauthorized responses to detect missing checks.\n5. Confirm and document the access-control gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API endpoint fails to enforce proper authorization, allowing access/actions the caller isn’t entitled to (broken access control).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Check if API allows authentication with both query parameter and header simultaneously": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s auth/authorization requirement and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control in this check (auth enforcement, token validation, authorization).\n3. Attempt access without auth, with another user’s token, and with a tampered token.\n4. Compare responses to detect missing/weak enforcement.\n5. Confirm and document the authentication/authorization gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s authentication/authorization control described here is missing or weak, allowing unauthorized access.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API key in URL parameter leaks to server logs and Referer headers": "Steps:\n\n1. Find where the token/key is passed and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Check whether it appears in the URL/query string (?api_key=..., ?token=...) instead of a header.\n3. Tokens in URLs leak via browser history, the Referer header on outbound links, proxies, and server access logs.\n4. Trigger a page with third-party resources and confirm the token appears in the outbound Referer.\n5. Confirm the token is exposed via URL/logs/Referer (recommend Authorization header + no secrets in URLs).\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key travels in the URL (or is logged/leaked via Referer), exposing it through history, logs, and proxies.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Verify if API token binding prevents token theft and replay": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain the API token/key and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Decode it and review: expiration, transport (HTTPS), storage (not in URL/JS/localStorage), signature strength, scope.\n3. Test replay after logout, tampering claims, and use over plain HTTP.\n4. Check for rotation/revocation support.\n5. Confirm and document the specific token weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key is weak in the aspect described (expiration, transport, storage, signing, scope, or revocation), risking theft or forgery.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test if API supports proof-of-possession tokens for enhanced security": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain the API token/key and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Decode it and review: expiration, transport (HTTPS), storage (not in URL/JS/localStorage), signature strength, scope.\n3. Test replay after logout, tampering claims, and use over plain HTTP.\n4. Check for rotation/revocation support.\n5. Confirm and document the specific token weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key is weak in the aspect described (expiration, transport, storage, signing, scope, or revocation), risking theft or forgery.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
