  "Check if API authentication uses different keys for different environments": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s auth/authorization requirement and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control in this check (auth enforcement, token validation, authorization).\n3. Attempt access without auth, with another user’s token, and with a tampered token.\n4. Compare responses to detect missing/weak enforcement.\n5. Confirm and document the authentication/authorization gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s authentication/authorization control described here is missing or weak, allowing unauthorized access.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API allows password authentication without brute force protection": "Steps:\n\n1. Identify the sensitive endpoint (the) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Send a rapid burst of requests (Burp Intruder / a script) and count how many succeed before any 429/lockout.\n3. Check whether limiting is per-user (better) or per-IP, and whether it resets too quickly.\n4. For auth/OTP/reset endpoints, no limit = brute force; for data endpoints = scraping/DoS.\n5. Confirm the absence (or ineffectiveness) of rate limiting.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe endpoint enforces no effective rate limiting, enabling brute force, credential attacks, scraping, or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Verify if API token signing algorithm matches expected algorithm strictly": "Steps:\n\n1. Grab a JWT and decode its header/payload.\n2. Test alg:none (strip signature) and algorithm confusion (RS256→HS256 signing with the public key).\n3. Tamper claims (sub, role, exp) and see if the server accepts the modified token.\n4. Test a weak HMAC secret with jwt_tool / hashcat, and kid injection/path traversal.\n5. Confirm any accepted forged/tampered token.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API accepts JWTs with weak/misconfigured signature validation (alg:none, algorithm confusion, weak secret, kid abuse), allowing token forgery.\n\nSeverity: High–Critical",
  "Test if API authentication bypass is possible via path confusion": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s auth/authorization requirement and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control in this check (auth enforcement, token validation, authorization).\n3. Attempt access without auth, with another user’s token, and with a tampered token.\n4. Compare responses to detect missing/weak enforcement.\n5. Confirm and document the authentication/authorization gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s authentication/authorization control described here is missing or weak, allowing unauthorized access.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Check if API key permissions are granular enough to enforce least privilege": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain the API token/key and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Decode it and review: expiration, transport (HTTPS), storage (not in URL/JS/localStorage), signature strength, scope.\n3. Test replay after logout, tampering claims, and use over plain HTTP.\n4. Check for rotation/revocation support.\n5. Confirm and document the specific token weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key is weak in the aspect described (expiration, transport, storage, signing, scope, or revocation), risking theft or forgery.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test if API token can be used to impersonate other users via claim modification": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain the API token/key and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Decode it and review: expiration, transport (HTTPS), storage (not in URL/JS/localStorage), signature strength, scope.\n3. Test replay after logout, tampering claims, and use over plain HTTP.\n4. Check for rotation/revocation support.\n5. Confirm and document the specific token weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key is weak in the aspect described (expiration, transport, storage, signing, scope, or revocation), risking theft or forgery.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
