  "Verify if API returns consistent error messages that do not expose internal structure": "Steps:\n\n1. Take the endpoint’s expected schema and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Send inputs that violate it — focus on malformed and unexpected inputs.\n3. Observe whether the API rejects with a clean 400 or mis-handles it (500, type juggling, injection, or silent acceptance).\n4. Mismatched/greedy validation is the root of injection, DoS, and logic bugs — chain into those where accepted.\n5. Confirm the API accepts/mishandles input that violates its own schema.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t strictly validate input against its schema (type/length/range/structure), allowing malformed data through — a precursor to injection, DoS, and logic flaws.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test if API returns generic error messages without exposing internal details": "Steps:\n\n1. Trigger errors on the endpoint: malformed JSON, wrong types, missing required fields, huge values, bad auth, unknown routes.\n2. Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it. Then read each error response body and status code.\n3. Check whether errors are generic and consistent, or leak internals / differ in a way that aids enumeration.\n4. Verify appropriate status codes (400 vs 401 vs 403 vs 404 vs 500) — inconsistent codes can leak existence/permissions.\n5. Confirm whether any error path reveals internal detail.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI error responses are inconsistent or reveal internal details / distinguish valid vs invalid resources, aiding attackers.\n\nSeverity: Low–Medium",
  "Check if API error responses do not include stack traces or debug information": "Steps:\n\n1. Send inputs designed to break the endpoint: bad types, malformed JSON/XML, SQL metacharacters ('), oversized payloads, and unexpected routes.\n2. Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it. Read the full error response.\n3. Look specifically for stack traces (class names, method paths, framework internals) in the body, headers, or status.\n4. Force a 500 (type confusion, division, null) to surface unhandled-exception output.\n5. Collect every internal detail leaked to build a picture of the backend.\n6. Recommend generic errors + server-side logging of the detail instead.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI error responses disclose stack traces (class names, method paths, framework internals), giving an attacker internal information useful for further attacks.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Verify if API returns appropriate HTTP status codes for different error scenarios": "Steps:\n\n1. Trigger errors on the endpoint: malformed JSON, wrong types, missing required fields, huge values, bad auth, unknown routes.\n2. Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it. Then read each error response body and status code.\n3. Check whether errors are generic and consistent, or leak internals / differ in a way that aids enumeration.\n4. Verify appropriate status codes (400 vs 401 vs 403 vs 404 vs 500) — inconsistent codes can leak existence/permissions.\n5. Confirm whether any error path reveals internal detail.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI error responses are inconsistent or reveal internal details / distinguish valid vs invalid resources, aiding attackers.\n\nSeverity: Low–Medium",
