  "Verify if API error responses do not assist attackers in understanding internal architecture": "Steps:\n\n1. Send inputs designed to break the endpoint: bad types, malformed JSON/XML, SQL metacharacters ('), oversized payloads, and unexpected routes.\n2. Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it. Read the full error response.\n3. Look specifically for internal implementation details in the body, headers, or status.\n4. Force a 500 (type confusion, division, null) to surface unhandled-exception output.\n5. Collect every internal detail leaked to build a picture of the backend.\n6. Recommend generic errors + server-side logging of the detail instead.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI error responses disclose internal implementation details, giving an attacker internal information useful for further attacks.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test if API pagination prevents excessive data retrieval via large page sizes": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to a huge value (limit=1000000) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Check if API enforces maximum limit parameter value for pagination": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to a huge value (limit=1000000) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test if API pagination allows bypassing access controls by iterating through all pages": "Steps:\n\n1. Use a list endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Iterate through all pages (increment page/offset or follow cursors) and inspect whether results include other users’/tenants’ data.\n3. Some APIs enforce authorization on the first page but leak everything when you paginate deep.\n4. Automate collecting all pages and check for cross-scope records.\n5. Confirm access-control bypass via pagination.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nPagination can be iterated to retrieve records beyond the caller’s authorization scope (access-control bypass via paging).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Verify if API cursor-based pagination tokens are cryptographically protected": "Steps:\n\n1. Capture a paginated response and note the next/prev cursor token.\n2. Decode it (often base64 of an id/offset):\necho '<cursor>' | base64 -d\n3. Tamper the decoded value to jump to or enumerate records outside your page/scope, then re-encode and send.\n4. If the cursor isn’t signed/encrypted, you can forge cursors (IDOR via pagination).\n5. Confirm access to records you shouldn’t reach via a crafted cursor.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nPagination cursors are decodable/tamperable (not cryptographically protected), so an attacker forges cursors to reach records outside their scope.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test if API offset-based pagination allows deep offset DoS attacks": "Steps:\n\n1. Test the list/filter endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try oversized/negative/zero/missing limit and offset values for DoS/over-retrieval.\n3. Decode and tamper any cursor token for IDOR.\n4. Iterate all pages checking for cross-user data leakage.\n5. Confirm the pagination/filtering weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s pagination/filtering lacks bounds or protection, enabling data over-exposure, cursor IDOR, or access-control bypass.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Check if API pagination parameters are validated for negative or zero values": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to a negative value (limit=-1, offset=-1) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
