  "Test if Swagger/OpenAPI documentation is accessible without authentication": "Steps:\n\n1. Probe common API-docs paths:\nfor p in swagger-ui swagger.json openapi.json api-docs v2/api-docs redoc graphql docs; do curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} /$p\\n\" https://target.com/$p; done\n2. Open any that return 200 without authentication.\n3. Public API docs expose the full endpoint/parameter map to anyone.\n4. Import the spec (OpenAPI/Swagger) into Burp/Postman for testing.\n5. Report internet-exposed documentation that reveals internal/admin operations.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI documentation (Swagger/OpenAPI/GraphQL) is accessible without authentication, handing an attacker the complete endpoint and schema map.\n\nSeverity: Low–Medium",
  "Check if API documentation exposes internal endpoints not meant for public use": "Steps:\n\n1. Probe common API-docs paths:\nfor p in swagger-ui swagger.json openapi.json api-docs v2/api-docs redoc graphql docs; do curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} /$p\\n\" https://target.com/$p; done\n2. Open any that return 200 without authentication.\n3. Public API docs expose the full endpoint/parameter map to anyone.\n4. Import the spec (OpenAPI/Swagger) into Burp/Postman for testing.\n5. Report internet-exposed documentation that reveals internal/admin operations.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI documentation (Swagger/OpenAPI/GraphQL) is accessible without authentication, handing an attacker the complete endpoint and schema map.\n\nSeverity: Low–Medium",
  "Test if API documentation reveals sensitive data models and field names": "Steps:\n\n1. Open the API documentation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Look for internal/admin/undocumented-in-UI endpoints, sensitive data models, field names, and version info it reveals.\n3. Call internal endpoints listed in the docs directly to check for missing auth.\n4. Assess whether the docs over-expose the backend design.\n5. Report documentation that leaks internal endpoints/data models.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI documentation exposes internal endpoints, sensitive data models/field names, or other design detail useful to an attacker.\n\nSeverity: Low–Medium",
  "Verify if API documentation includes default credentials or API keys": "Steps:\n\n1. Open the accessible API docs and read example requests, descriptions, and defaults.\n2. Search for embedded credentials/keys/tokens in examples:\ncurl -s https://target.com/openapi.json | grep -iE 'key|secret|token|password|Bearer '\n3. Docs sometimes ship real default credentials or example API keys that work.\n4. Test any credential/key found (authorized only) to confirm validity.\n5. Report leaked credentials/keys in documentation.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI documentation includes real/default credentials or API keys (in examples/defaults), directly leaking working secrets.\n\nSeverity: High",
