  "Test if API logs authentication failures for brute force detection": "Steps:\n\n1. Assess the API’s security logging for this scenario (auth failures, authz violations, forensic context).\n2. Trigger failed logins / access-denied / anomalous requests and determine whether they’re logged with enough context (who/what/when/where).\n3. Insufficient logging means attacks (brute force, access violations) go undetected.\n4. Check for tamper-resistance and that logs aren’t publicly reachable.\n5. Report gaps in security logging/monitoring for this event type.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t adequately log security-relevant events (auth/authz failures, anomalies), hampering detection and forensics (insufficient logging & monitoring).\n\nSeverity: Low",
  "Check if API logs contain sensitive data like passwords or tokens": "Steps:\n\n1. Review whether the API logs sensitive data (assess via any accessible logs, verbose responses, or source/config).\n2. Trigger auth/payment requests and check if full tokens/passwords/PANs/PII would be written to logs.\n3. If logs are reachable (recon: /logs, error.log, log aggregation UIs), confirm sensitive values appear.\n4. Sensitive data in logs = secondary exposure if logs leak or are over-shared.\n5. Report sensitive data written to logs (recommend masking/redaction).\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API logs sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII), so any log exposure or over-broad log access leaks credentials/personal data.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
