  "Test if API rate limiting is enforced on authentication endpoints": "Steps:\n\n1. Identify the sensitive endpoint (the) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Send a rapid burst of requests (Burp Intruder / a script) and count how many succeed before any 429/lockout.\n3. Check whether limiting is per-user (better) or per-IP, and whether it resets too quickly.\n4. For auth/OTP/reset endpoints, no limit = brute force; for data endpoints = scraping/DoS.\n5. Confirm the absence (or ineffectiveness) of rate limiting.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe endpoint enforces no effective rate limiting, enabling brute force, credential attacks, scraping, or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Check if API rate limiting applies per user rather than per IP address": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a rate-limited endpoint (hit it until 429) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try bypassing the limit by rotating the client key the limiter uses:\nX-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4\nX-Real-IP: 1.2.3.4\nX-Client-IP / X-Forwarded-Host\n3. Also vary: case/trailing slash in path, HTTP version, new session/token per request, and different API version paths.\n4. Use Burp Intruder / Turbo Intruder to measure true throughput vs the stated limit.\n5. Confirm you can exceed the intended limit.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe rate limit is keyed on a spoofable value (IP header) or bypassable via path/version/session variation, so brute force and abuse still work.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test if API rate limiting can be bypassed via X-Forwarded-For header manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a rate-limited endpoint (hit it until 429) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try bypassing the limit by rotating the client key the limiter uses:\nX-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4\nX-Real-IP: 1.2.3.4\nX-Client-IP / X-Forwarded-Host\n3. Also vary: case/trailing slash in path, HTTP version, new session/token per request, and different API version paths.\n4. Use Burp Intruder / Turbo Intruder to measure true throughput vs the stated limit.\n5. Confirm you can exceed the intended limit.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe rate limit is keyed on a spoofable value (IP header) or bypassable via path/version/session variation, so brute force and abuse still work.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
