  "Check if API rate limiting accounts for costs of AI/ML inference endpoints": "Steps:\n\n1. Identify the sensitive endpoint (the) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Send a rapid burst of requests (Burp Intruder / a script) and count how many succeed before any 429/lockout.\n3. Check whether limiting is per-user (better) or per-IP, and whether it resets too quickly.\n4. For auth/OTP/reset endpoints, no limit = brute force; for data endpoints = scraping/DoS.\n5. Confirm the absence (or ineffectiveness) of rate limiting.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe endpoint enforces no effective rate limiting, enabling brute force, credential attacks, scraping, or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test if API webhook URLs are validated to prevent SSRF attacks": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to internal/private ranges (http://127.0.0.1/, http://169.254.169.254/, http://10.0.0.1/).\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Check if API webhook delivery authenticates the receiving endpoint": "Steps:\n\n1. Attempt to register/modify a webhook and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try registering webhooks for objects/tenants you don’t own (change the id/tenant in the request).\n3. Check whether webhook creation requires proper authentication and per-object authorization.\n4. Test whether you can point another tenant’s events to your URL (data exfiltration).\n5. Confirm unauthorized webhook registration or cross-tenant delivery.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nWebhook registration lacks proper authentication/authorization, letting an attacker create webhooks or redirect other tenants’ events to attacker-controlled URLs.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API webhook payload signing prevents tampering and forgery": "Steps:\n\n1. Register a webhook and capture a delivered payload to your endpoint.\n2. Check whether it includes a signature header (X-Signature / X-Hub-Signature) and how it’s computed.\n3. Replay the payload with modified content and the same/absent signature to your receiver.\n4. If your receiver (or the API’s verification) accepts a tampered/unsigned payload, integrity isn’t enforced.\n5. Also test signature bypasses: empty signature, wrong algorithm, and timing/replay of old signed payloads.\n6. Confirm forged webhook events are accepted as genuine.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nWebhook payloads aren’t signed (or signatures aren’t verified), so an attacker can forge or tamper with webhook events.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Verify if API webhook URL validation blocks internal and private IP addresses": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to internal/private ranges (http://127.0.0.1/, http://169.254.169.254/, http://10.0.0.1/).\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test if API webhook registration requires proper authorization": "Steps:\n\n1. Attempt to register/modify a webhook and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try registering webhooks for objects/tenants you don’t own (change the id/tenant in the request).\n3. Check whether webhook creation requires proper authentication and per-object authorization.\n4. Test whether you can point another tenant’s events to your URL (data exfiltration).\n5. Confirm unauthorized webhook registration or cross-tenant delivery.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nWebhook registration lacks proper authentication/authorization, letting an attacker create webhooks or redirect other tenants’ events to attacker-controlled URLs.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Check if API webhook retry mechanism has exponential backoff with limit": "Steps:\n\n1. Register a webhook pointing to a slow/failing endpoint you control.\n2. Trigger many events and observe the retry behaviour.\n3. Check for exponential backoff and a retry cap — unbounded retries enable amplification/DoS against a third party.\n4. Assess whether an attacker can weaponise retries to flood a victim URL.\n5. Confirm missing backoff/retry limits.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nWebhook retries lack exponential backoff and a cap, enabling request amplification/DoS toward an attacker-chosen target.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test if API webhook delivery includes HMAC signature for verification": "Steps:\n\n1. Register a webhook and capture a delivered payload to your endpoint.\n2. Check whether it includes a signature header (X-Signature / X-Hub-Signature) and how it’s computed.\n3. Replay the payload with modified content and the same/absent signature to your receiver.\n4. If your receiver (or the API’s verification) accepts a tampered/unsigned payload, integrity isn’t enforced.\n5. Also test signature bypasses: empty signature, wrong algorithm, and timing/replay of old signed payloads.\n6. Confirm forged webhook events are accepted as genuine.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nWebhook payloads aren’t signed (or signatures aren’t verified), so an attacker can forge or tamper with webhook events.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
