  "Test for API field-level access control between versions": "Steps:\n\n1. Enumerate API versions (path /v1/,/v2/,/v3/ and version headers/Accept) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Take a secured action on the current version, then replay it on the older/deprecated version.\n3. Compare: does the old version skip auth, rate limits, or authorization the new one enforces?\n4. Test object access and privileged actions specifically on the legacy version.\n5. Confirm any security control that’s weaker/missing on an older version.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nDeprecated/older API versions remain reachable with weaker (or missing) security controls than the current version, so attackers use the legacy version to bypass fixes.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for API breaking change information disclosure": "Steps:\n\n1. Review the versioning scheme and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Enumerate all live versions and compare their security controls (auth, authz, rate limit, validation).\n3. Check that deprecated versions are sunset (warnings/blocked) and consistent across endpoints.\n4. Test manipulating the version selector to reach weaker versions.\n5. Confirm and document the versioning weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nAPI versioning is inconsistent or lets clients reach deprecated/weaker versions, undermining security controls.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
