  "Test for pagination bypass via large limit parameter": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to a huge value (limit=1000000) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test for pagination bypass via negative offset parameter": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to a negative value (limit=-1, offset=-1) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test for pagination bypass via zero offset parameter": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to zero (limit=0, offset=0) and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test for pagination bypass via missing limit parameter": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a list endpoint with pagination (limit/offset/page/size) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the pagination parameter to the parameter omitted entirely and send.\n3. Check whether the API caps the value or returns the entire dataset / errors out (DoS or data dump).\n4. Large/negative/zero/missing limits can bypass the intended page cap or exhaust resources.\n5. Confirm excessive data retrieval or a resource-exhaustion condition.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce a maximum page size (accepts oversized/negative/zero/missing limits), enabling bulk data exfiltration or DoS.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test for pagination bypass via cursor manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Capture a paginated response and note the next/prev cursor token.\n2. Decode it (often base64 of an id/offset):\necho '<cursor>' | base64 -d\n3. Tamper the decoded value to jump to or enumerate records outside your page/scope, then re-encode and send.\n4. If the cursor isn’t signed/encrypted, you can forge cursors (IDOR via pagination).\n5. Confirm access to records you shouldn’t reach via a crafted cursor.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nPagination cursors are decodable/tamperable (not cryptographically protected), so an attacker forges cursors to reach records outside their scope.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test for pagination bypass via page number manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Test the list/filter endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try oversized/negative/zero/missing limit and offset values for DoS/over-retrieval.\n3. Decode and tamper any cursor token for IDOR.\n4. Iterate all pages checking for cross-user data leakage.\n5. Confirm the pagination/filtering weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s pagination/filtering lacks bounds or protection, enabling data over-exposure, cursor IDOR, or access-control bypass.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
  "Test for pagination bypass via after/before parameter manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Test the list/filter endpoint and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try oversized/negative/zero/missing limit and offset values for DoS/over-retrieval.\n3. Decode and tamper any cursor token for IDOR.\n4. Iterate all pages checking for cross-user data leakage.\n5. Confirm the pagination/filtering weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API’s pagination/filtering lacks bounds or protection, enabling data over-exposure, cursor IDOR, or access-control bypass.\n\nSeverity: Medium",
