  "Test for mass assignment via role field in user update API": "Steps:\n\n1. Find the user update request that updates/creates the object (e.g. PUT/PATCH/POST) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Note which fields the client normally sends. The `role` field is NOT one a normal user should control.\n3. Add the field to the JSON body and set a privileged value:\n{ ... , \"role\": \"admin\" }\n4. Resend and check the response + a follow-up GET to see if `role` was actually saved/changed.\n5. If the direct field is filtered, try variants: nested (\"user\":{\"role\":\"admin\"}), snake vs camelCase, arrays, and duplicate keys.\n6. Confirm real impact by using the account/object afterwards (e.g. you now have elevated access).\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API binds the `role` attribute straight from the request body without an allow-list, so an attacker sets a field they shouldn’t control — here a privilege/security-sensitive field, enabling escalation or fraud.\n\nSeverity: High–Critical",
  "Test for mass assignment via isAdmin field in registration API": "Steps:\n\n1. Find the registration request that updates/creates the object (e.g. PUT/PATCH/POST) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Note which fields the client normally sends. The `isAdmin` field is NOT one a normal user should control.\n3. Add the field to the JSON body and set a privileged value:\n{ ... , \"isAdmin\": \"admin\" }\n4. Resend and check the response + a follow-up GET to see if `isAdmin` was actually saved/changed.\n5. If the direct field is filtered, try variants: nested (\"user\":{\"isAdmin\":\"admin\"}), snake vs camelCase, arrays, and duplicate keys.\n6. Confirm real impact by using the account/object afterwards (e.g. you now have elevated access).\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API binds the `isAdmin` attribute straight from the request body without an allow-list, so an attacker sets a field they shouldn’t control — here a privilege/security-sensitive field, enabling escalation or fraud.\n\nSeverity: High–Critical",
