  "Test for mass assignment via email_verified field in account API": "Steps:\n\n1. Find the account request that updates/creates the object (e.g. PUT/PATCH/POST) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Note which fields the client normally sends. The `email_verified` field is NOT one a normal user should control.\n3. Add the field to the JSON body and set a privileged value:\n{ ... , \"email_verified\": true }\n4. Resend and check the response + a follow-up GET to see if `email_verified` was actually saved/changed.\n5. If the direct field is filtered, try variants: nested (\"user\":{\"email_verified\":true}), snake vs camelCase, arrays, and duplicate keys.\n6. Confirm real impact by using the account/object afterwards (e.g. you now have elevated access).\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API binds the `email_verified` attribute straight from the request body without an allow-list, so an attacker sets a field they shouldn’t control — here a privilege/security-sensitive field, enabling escalation or fraud.\n\nSeverity: High–Critical",
  "Test for mass assignment via subscription_tier in billing API": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a create/update API request and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Enumerate extra parameters the API might bind: role, isAdmin, permission, group, verified, status, price, owner, id.\n3. Add these fields to the JSON body one by one with privileged values and resend.\n4. Check via a follow-up read whether any injected field was persisted.\n5. Try nested objects, alternate casing, and duplicate keys to bypass naive filters.\n6. Confirm impact (privilege escalation, price/ownership tampering) with the modified object.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API auto-binds request-body fields to the object without an allow-list, letting an attacker set sensitive attributes (mass assignment / over-posting).\n\nSeverity: High",
