  "Test for BOLA (Broken Object Level Authorization) on all GET endpoints": "Steps:\n\n1. Create two accounts (A and B). As A, perform the operation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Find the object identifier in the request (path/query/body/header), e.g. /api/resource/{id}.\n3. Swap A’s object id for B’s and replay the GET request.\n4. If you read/modify/delete B’s object, object-level authorization is missing (BOLA/IDOR).\n5. Try id formats: sequential ints, UUIDs harvested from responses, encoded ids, and nested ids.\n6. Confirm cross-user access and note the data/action exposed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API checks that the object exists but not that the caller owns it, so changing the id on a GET request accesses another user’s resource (BOLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for BOLA on all PUT/PATCH endpoints for resource modification": "Steps:\n\n1. Create two accounts (A and B). As A, perform the operation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Find the object identifier in the request (path/query/body/header), e.g. /api/resource/{id}.\n3. Swap A’s object id for B’s and replay the PUT/PATCH request.\n4. If you read/modify/delete B’s object, object-level authorization is missing (BOLA/IDOR).\n5. Try id formats: sequential ints, UUIDs harvested from responses, encoded ids, and nested ids.\n6. Confirm cross-user access and note the data/action exposed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API checks that the object exists but not that the caller owns it, so changing the id on a PUT/PATCH request accesses another user’s resource (BOLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for BOLA on all DELETE endpoints for resource deletion": "Steps:\n\n1. Create two accounts (A and B). As A, perform the operation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Find the object identifier in the request (path/query/body/header), e.g. /api/resource/{id}.\n3. Swap A’s object id for B’s and replay the DELETE request.\n4. If you read/modify/delete B’s object, object-level authorization is missing (BOLA/IDOR).\n5. Try id formats: sequential ints, UUIDs harvested from responses, encoded ids, and nested ids.\n6. Confirm cross-user access and note the data/action exposed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API checks that the object exists but not that the caller owns it, so changing the id on a DELETE request accesses another user’s resource (BOLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for BOLA on all POST endpoints for resource creation": "Steps:\n\n1. Create two accounts (A and B). As A, perform the operation and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Find the object identifier in the request (path/query/body/header), e.g. /api/resource/{id}.\n3. Swap A’s object id for B’s and replay the POST request.\n4. If you read/modify/delete B’s object, object-level authorization is missing (BOLA/IDOR).\n5. Try id formats: sequential ints, UUIDs harvested from responses, encoded ids, and nested ids.\n6. Confirm cross-user access and note the data/action exposed.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API checks that the object exists but not that the caller owns it, so changing the id on a POST request accesses another user’s resource (BOLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for BPLA (Broken Property Level Authorization) on read operations": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s intended audience/permission and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Attempt the action as an unauthorized user (no auth, other user, lower role).\n3. Tamper object ids and try privileged methods/endpoints.\n4. Compare authorized vs unauthorized responses to detect missing checks.\n5. Confirm and document the access-control gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API endpoint fails to enforce proper authorization, allowing access/actions the caller isn’t entitled to (broken access control).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for BPLA on write operations for property modification": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the endpoint’s intended audience/permission and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Attempt the action as an unauthorized user (no auth, other user, lower role).\n3. Tamper object ids and try privileged methods/endpoints.\n4. Compare authorized vs unauthorized responses to detect missing checks.\n5. Confirm and document the access-control gap.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API endpoint fails to enforce proper authorization, allowing access/actions the caller isn’t entitled to (broken access control).\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for privilege escalation via API role manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Enumerate privileged/admin endpoints (from docs, JS, or guessing) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. As a low-privilege user, call the admin/privileged endpoint or use a higher-privilege HTTP method (PUT/DELETE).\n3. Also try forcing methods the UI doesn’t expose (X-HTTP-Method-Override, different verbs).\n4. If the action succeeds without the required role, function-level authorization is broken (BFLA).\n5. Confirm privilege escalation with the performed action.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API doesn’t enforce role/function-level authorization, so a low-privilege user invokes privileged operations (BFLA).\n\nSeverity: High",
