  "Test for SSRF via webhook URL configuration parameter": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to internal/private ranges (http://127.0.0.1/, http://169.254.169.254/, http://10.0.0.1/).\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for SSRF via webhook URL redirect following": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to a public URL you control that 302-redirects to http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/.\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for SSRF via webhook URL DNS rebinding": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to a DNS-rebinding host that first resolves to a public IP then to 169.254.169.254 / 127.0.0.1.\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test for SSRF via webhook URL IPv6 address manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Go to the webhook/callback configuration and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Set the webhook URL to IPv6 loopback/link-local (http://[::1]/, http://[::ffff:169.254.169.254]/).\n3. Trigger delivery (save/test webhook or the event that fires it).\n4. Use a Burp Collaborator / interactsh URL as the payload target and watch for a callback (DNS/HTTP) that proves the request left the server.\n5. Try bypasses if blocked: decimal/octal/hex IPs, http://127.0.0.1.nip.io, added ports, uppercase scheme, and the redirect/DNS-rebinding tricks above.\n6. Confirm SSRF by retrieving internal data (metadata creds, internal service response) or a server-originated callback.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe webhook URL is fetched server-side without validating against internal/private/metadata addresses (and without blocking redirects/rebinding), so an attacker reaches internal resources — SSRF.\n\nSeverity: High",
