  "Test API token security: API key rate limiting bypass vulnerability": "Steps:\n\n1. Find a rate-limited endpoint (hit it until 429) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Try bypassing the limit by rotating the client key the limiter uses:\nX-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4\nX-Real-IP: 1.2.3.4\nX-Client-IP / X-Forwarded-Host\n3. Also vary: case/trailing slash in path, HTTP version, new session/token per request, and different API version paths.\n4. Use Burp Intruder / Turbo Intruder to measure true throughput vs the stated limit.\n5. Confirm you can exceed the intended limit.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe rate limit is keyed on a spoofable value (IP header) or bypassable via path/version/session variation, so brute force and abuse still work.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
  "Test API token security: API key scope escalation via parameter manipulation": "Steps:\n\n1. Map the OAuth2 flow (authorize/token endpoints) and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Test the specific control: client_secret validation on token requests, scope enforcement, refresh-token rotation, redirect_uri validation, PKCE.\n3. Try tampering scope, reusing refresh tokens, and using loose redirect_uri matches to capture codes/tokens.\n4. Check whether tokens are bound to the client and can’t be replayed elsewhere.\n5. Confirm any weakness that yields excess scope or token theft.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe OAuth2 implementation weakly validates client credentials/scope/redirect_uri/refresh tokens, enabling scope escalation or token theft.\n\nSeverity: High",
  "Test API token security: API key type confusion between read/write permissions": "Steps:\n\n1. Obtain the API token/key and Capture the request in Burp Suite (Proxy → HTTP history) and send it to Repeater so you can tamper and replay it.\n2. Decode it and review: expiration, transport (HTTPS), storage (not in URL/JS/localStorage), signature strength, scope.\n3. Test replay after logout, tampering claims, and use over plain HTTP.\n4. Check for rotation/revocation support.\n5. Confirm and document the specific token weakness in this check.\n\nVulnerable When:\n\nThe API token/key is weak in the aspect described (expiration, transport, storage, signing, scope, or revocation), risking theft or forgery.\n\nSeverity: Medium–High",
